{"title":"How plants make their own food using sunlight","key_idea":"Plants use sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide together in their leaves to make glucose and release oxygen, but animals cannot do this.","method":["Identify the three ingredients a plant needs: sunlight from the sun, water from the soil through roots, and carbon dioxide gas from the air.","Recall that these ingredients meet inside the green parts of leaves called chloroplasts.","Understand that sunlight energy powers a chemical change that turns water and carbon dioxide into glucose (food) and oxygen.","Remember that animals eat food made by plants or other animals, but plants make their own food."],"worked_example":{"problem":"A plant in a sunny window receives light and water. What does the plant make, and what does it release?","steps":["The plant has sunlight from the window and water from the soil.","Inside the chloroplasts of the leaves, these combine with carbon dioxide from the air.","The plant makes glucose to use as food for growth and staying alive.","The plant releases oxygen as a waste product."],"answer":"The plant makes glucose (food) and releases oxygen."},"key_terms":[{"term":"Chloroplasts","meaning":"tiny green structures inside leaf cells where sunlight energy is used to make food"},{"term":"Glucose","meaning":"sugar that plants make and use as food for energy and growth"},{"term":"Carbon dioxide","meaning":"a gas in the air that plants take in through tiny holes in their leaves"}],"common_mistakes":["Thinking that plants get all their food from soil—actually they make it using sunlight and air.","Forgetting that oxygen is released by plants during this process, not used up."],"remember":"Plants make their own food using light, water, and air; animals must eat food they cannot make themselves.","quick_check":[{"q":"Name the three things a plant needs to make food.","a":"Sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide."},{"q":"Where inside the leaf does the food-making process happen?","a":"In the chloroplasts."}],"tell_your_child":"Plants are amazing because they can catch sunlight and turn it into food—something animals like us can never do! That is why plants are so important: they make food and oxygen that all other living things need."}
